  <h2 id="learn">How to learn using a placement cell cheaply, and what proof counts</h2>

  <p>
    Using a placement cell is learned by doing real work, so the cheapest route is also the best. A paid course is worth it only when it gives feedback and finished work that you cannot get free.
  </p>
  <h3>Free and low-cost routes first</h3>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Free resume templates and mock interview videos.</li>
    <li>Free aptitude practice sets.</li>
    <li>Free public placement reports to compare.</li>
    <li>A senior who gives honest interview feedback.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>How to judge a paid course</h3>
  <p>
    Judge each course, not the platform. Ask what you will build, who reviews it, which role graduates reached, how current the material is and what the refund terms are, in writing.
  </p>
  <h3>Your week, and a first small test</h3>
  <p>
    A starting plan is about ten hours a week: three of lessons and seven of practice on one project. Do not pay for an expensive programme before this habit holds.
  </p>
  <p>
    This week, ask the cell three specific questions and write down the answers.
  </p>
  <h3>Building a high-income skill portfolio beside the credential</h3>
  <p>
    A degree has a place, and the weak spot is relying on it alone. A skill mix of one core skill, a second skill, communication, a niche and a clear offer builds a high-income skill portfolio, and a niche service can bring an earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ask">Ten questions to ask your placement cell</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Question</th>
          <th>Why it matters</th>
          <th>Look for</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Question"><strong>Which companies visited in the last two years and for which roles?</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it matters">Shows real patterns</td>
          <td data-label="Look for">Names, roles and numbers</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Question"><strong>How many students got offers, and how many accepted?</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it matters">Shows outcomes</td>
          <td data-label="Look for">Counts, not only percentages</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Question"><strong>What skills did selection tests check?</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it matters">Shows what to prepare</td>
          <td data-label="Look for">Test names and sample formats</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Question"><strong>What training does the cell offer and when?</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it matters">Helps you plan</td>
          <td data-label="Look for">A dated schedule</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Question"><strong>How are students shortlisted for each drive?</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it matters">Fairness and planning</td>
          <td data-label="Look for">Written criteria</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Question"><strong>What support is there for students not placed?</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it matters">Plan B</td>
          <td data-label="Look for">Off-campus guidance</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Question"><strong>Can alumni connect with us?</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it matters">Networking</td>
          <td data-label="Look for">Named alumni or events</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

